hat I believe it's  gang leaders and only gang leaders that  can actually transform communities why  is that they're so disproportionately  powerful but that power can be harnessed  for good I think the one unconventional  thing that people find is you're going  to pay someone who's in a gang been in a  gang to go to school right it would be  great if everybody could do exactly the  right thing all the time  none of us do that when you offer them a  different opportunity and you say we  will invest in you to make different  choices they're desperate to do that  they want a way out they just need an  excuse and an opportunity Carlton can  relate having made poor choices as a  teenager after his parents divorce I  immediately sort of fell off the cliff  started selling drugs breaking into  houses like just sort of went wild yeah  and I would get arrested and my  college-educated white mother would come  down to the police station and like make  the police apologize to me and send me  home  he says the philosophy behind the  program Boston nun cornered is based on  three core principles hire former gang  members there's different scholarships  out there you want to try to get you  insist on high expectations from  students and invest in their future in  other words don't be afraid to put your  money where your mouth is to give these  leaders an opportunity to make positive  choices and if you just do those three  simple things your city will change what  did you think when you first heard about  it I said it's about time Boston Police  Commissioner William grass the first  african-american to lead his force sees  this as an extension of the department's  community policing enacted citywide we  want to reach the individuals that are  having challenges and then help them out  so you actually pay in the gang  remember or are you paying Spain a  future right so you say gang's right we  pay for the lawyers incarceration the  upkeep no this is an investment into  someone's future to change their life  for the better and it'll be a return  investment for us the community like  Antonio Franklin using the investment  made in him to lay a foundation for his  future and his families you have a  two-year-old yes what does this program  mean in terms of him an example you're  able to set for him a role model it  shows that you know I don't want my son  to look back on my life  Google me and see that oh my father was  just somebody who was in the streets I  wanted to see that my father you know he  was in the streets but he came home and  he really did good and I just do good he  did good for the betterment of everybody  around him that's who I want to be an  Antonia wants to use the degree when he  gets it to start a youth program and  help others this is the whole idea  behind Boston on cornered one Domino at  a time Antonio knew Francisco in the  neighborhood they were both in the gang  he gets out he sees that he's doing  better hey maybe I can do this you need  that leg up you need the help Marc talk  to us about the name everyone has been  cornered in at some point and everyone  needs help to get out of that corner and  that's behind Boswell and that's the  more important part of it though is not  just doing it for yourself doing it for  the next generation and doing it for the  kids so they can say like what what was  daddy what was mommy yeah and they do  and they they point out also the cost of  incarceration and repeated incarceration  is more than what is being invested in  these people each year and that's  something to consider as well here's to  Marc and chief Willy I like that because  everyone behind that program
 

